Suduko Rules:

A number cannot repeat in the same row, same column, OR same 3×3 box.

class Sudoku {

    private int[][] board;

    Sudoku() {
        board = new int[9][9];
    }

    boolean placeNumber(int row, int col, int num) {

        if (board[row][col] != 0)
            return false;

        if (!isValid(row, col, num))
            return false;

        board[row][col] = num;
        return true;
    }

    boolean isValid(int row, int col, int num) {

        // Check row
        for (int c = 0; c < 9; c++) {
            if (board[row][c] == num)
                return false;
        }

        // Check column
        for (int r = 0; r < 9; r++) {
            if (board[r][col] == num)
                return false;
        }

        // Check 3 x 3 box
        int startRow = (row / 3) * 3;
        int startCol = (col / 3) * 3;

        for (int r = startRow; r < startRow + 3; r++) {
            for (int c = startCol; c < startCol + 3; c++) {
                if (board[r][c] == num)
                    return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}


The above isValid() scans the row/column/box every time → O(9), which is effectively constant for Sudoku.

If you scan the row + column + 3×3 box for every placement:

Row scan → O(9)
Column scan → O(9)
Box scan → O(9)

Total:

O(9 + 9 + 9) = O(27) = O(1)

Because Sudoku's board size is fixed at 9 × 9.

If we generalize Sudoku to an N × N board:

Row → O(N)
Column → O(N)
Box → O(N)

So validation becomes O(N).





Optimisation

This is similar to TIC TAC GAME - check design tic-tac-toe
 For Sudoku optimized validation, use the same idea as Tic-Tac-Toe: maintain the state instead of scanning the row/column/box every time.


// rows[row][num] = whether num already exists in that row
    private boolean[][] rows = new boolean[9][10];

// cols[col][num] = whether num already exists in that column
    private boolean[][] cols = new boolean[9][10];

// boxes[box][num] = whether num already exists in that 3x3 box
    private boolean[][] boxes = new boolean[9][10];




